Nine o'clock. A meeting with new people. Everyone goes around the table and says their name and their role. The person next to you says their name. Then it's your turn. You say yours. And by the end of the meeting, you have no idea what the person next to you was called. They were right next to you. This has a name: the next-in-line effect. In one study, students sat around a table and took turns reading words out loud. Later they had to remember every word they had heard. They remembered their own words very well, nearly nine in ten. But the words read just before their turn, and just after it, were remembered worst of all. The closer a word came to their own turn, the more likely it was to vanish. Here's why. When your turn is coming, your brain is busy getting ready. What will I say? How will I sound? There isn't room left to store what the person next to you is saying. Then, while you speak and calm down afterward, the same thing happens again. And names have a second problem. Researchers have found that it is much harder to remember that a person's name is Baker than to remember that a person is a baker. It's the same word. The reason is meaning. A baker connects to a hundred things you already know: bread, early mornings, flour on their hands. Mr. Baker connects to almost nothing. A name is a label with no story attached. Your brain is built to keep stories. So if you want to keep a name, give it a story. Link it to something. Say it back once, out loud, after your own turn is over. Again, notice what your brain is doing. It isn't broken. It's protecting what matters most to it in that moment: your own turn, and things that connect to what you already know. But what about the things you work hard to learn? Surely those stay. Not so fast.[0m
